    <h2 id="single-tier" class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink dark:text-on-primary">1. The Single-Tier Corporate Tax System Explained</h2>
    <p>
      Under Section 13E of the <a href="https://www.iras.gov.sg/taxes/corporate-income-tax" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-link hover:underline">Singapore Income Tax Act</a>, Singapore operates a sophisticated 1-tier corporate tax framework. This system was introduced to simplify the tax code, reduce administrative burdens, and make the city-state exceptionally attractive to international investors and local entrepreneurs alike.
    </p>
    <p>
      In many jurisdictions across the world, corporate profits face a dreaded phenomenon known as "double taxation." First, the company is taxed on its corporate earnings. Then, when the remaining profits are distributed to shareholders as dividends, those individuals are taxed again on their personal income tax returns. Singapore eliminates this entirely.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under the single-tier system, corporate profits are taxed once at the corporate level at a flat headline rate of 17%. Once that tax is paid, any dividend payouts distributed to shareholders are completely tax-exempt. This means shareholders receive their dividends clean, with no further tax obligations, no complex franking credits to calculate, and no withholding tax issues on normal dividends. For startup founders looking to eventually draw wealth from their successful ventures, this structure is a massive financial advantage that directly preserves personal wealth.
    </p>

    <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1486406146926-c627a92ad1ab?w=1200&q=80" alt="Modern corporate skyscraper building in Singapore CBD" class="my-8 w-full rounded-xl object-cover max-h-96 shadow-md" loading="lazy" />

    <h2 id="sote-exemption" class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink dark:text-on-primary">2. In-Depth: Start-Up Tax Exemption Scheme (SOTE)</h2>
    <p>
      To aggressively encourage entrepreneurship and support small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) in their critical early years, the government introduced the Tax Exemption Scheme for New Start-Up Companies, commonly referred to as SOTE. If your company qualifies, the effective tax rate on your first few hundred thousand dollars of profit can drop to single digits.
    </p>
    
    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-6">Qualifying Conditions for SOTE</h3>
    <p>
      Not every new company automatically receives SOTE. To prevent abuse of the system (such as constantly incorporating new companies just to claim exemptions), IRAS has strict qualifying criteria. Your company must meet all three of the following conditions in the Year of Assessment (YA) to qualify:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-muted mt-4">
      <li><strong>Incorporation:</strong> The company must be incorporated in Singapore.</li>
      <li><strong>Tax Residency:</strong> The company must be a tax resident in Singapore for that YA. This generally means the control and management of the business is exercised in Singapore.</li>
      <li><strong>Shareholding Structure:</strong> The company's total share capital must be beneficially held directly by no more than 20 shareholders throughout the basis period for that YA. Furthermore, all shareholders must be individuals, OR at least one shareholder must be an individual holding at least 10% of the issued ordinary shares of the company.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4">
      <em>Note: Property development and investment holding companies do not qualify for SOTE, as these sectors are not considered the target demographic for this specific startup incubation incentive.</em>
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-6">How SOTE is Calculated</h3>
    <p>
      For qualifying companies, the exemption applies for their first 3 consecutive Years of Assessment. The current exemption tiers are incredibly beneficial:
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
      <table class="min-w-full border border-hairline text-left text-sm dark:border-overlay-lighter">
        <thead class="bg-canvas-soft font-bold text-ink dark:bg-surface dark:text-on-primary">
          <tr>
            <th class="border p-3">Normal Chargeable Income Tier</th>
            <th class="border p-3">Exemption Rate (%)</th>
            <th class="border p-3">Exempted Income Amount</th>
            <th class="border p-3">Taxable Balance</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline dark:divide-overlay-lighter">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-semibold">First S$100,000</td>
            <td class="p-3 font-bold text-emerald-600">75%</td>
            <td class="p-3">S$75,000</td>
            <td class="p-3">S$25,000 (Taxed at 17% = S$4,250)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
